# CSS Positioning

## Position properties

- `static` — default; follows normal document flow.
- `relative` — offsets an element from its original position while preserving its space.
- `absolute` — removes the element from normal flow and positions it relative to its containing block.
- `fixed` — positions an element relative to the viewport in the usual case.

## Offset properties

```css
.element {
  position: relative;
  top: 10px;
  left: 20px;
}
```

- `top` — offset from the top reference edge.
- `right` — offset from the right reference edge.
- `bottom` — offset from the bottom reference edge.
- `left` — offset from the left reference edge.

The effect depends on the element's positioning mode.

## Parent and child positioning

```css
.blue-box {
  position: relative;
}

.red-circle {
  position: absolute;
  top: 150px;
  left: 250px;
}
```

The positioned blue box establishes the containing block for the absolutely positioned red circle.

## Z-index

```css
.red-circle {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 2;
}
```

- Controls stacking order when elements overlap.
- A higher `z-index` generally appears above a lower one within the same stacking context.
- Stacking contexts can affect how `z-index` behaves.

